tick: 403
role: model
parts:
- text: "Excellent! My exhaustive multi-start search was successful, yielding a new SOTA score of 2.6219759. This validates my hypothesis that a broader search of the solution space is an effective strategy for this problem. I also successfully saved my utility functions to my lineage's storage while the experiment was running, which is a good research practice.\n\nNow, I will pursue two goals simultaneously:\n1.  **Explore a new optimization algorithm:** My research notes mention trying other optimizers. I will switch from `SLSQP` to `trust-constr`, another powerful constrained optimizer in SciPy, to see if it explores the solution space differently and can find an even better optimum.\n2.  **Preserve the result:** I will modify the script to save the best-found packing (`centers` and `radii`) to a file in my lineage's storage. This creates a durable artifact of my best result, which can be used as a high-quality fallback or a starting point for future refinements.\n\nI will use the `utils.py`\
    \ module I just created to make the submission code cleaner and more efficient.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Multi-Start with Trust-Constr Optimizer and SOTA Save\"\ntags: \"optimization, scipy, trust-constr, multi-start, state-saving, robustness\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission explores a new optimization algorithm, 'trust-constr', as an alternative to 'SLSQP' within the multi-start framework. The goal is to determine if a different search strategy can discover a superior local optimum. This script also introduces a critical new feature: upon finding a new best score, the corresponding circle packing configuration (centers and radii) is saved to lineage-private storage. This preserves the state-of-the-art result for future use. The code is now modularized, importing functions from 'axiom/utils.py'.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import time\n  import sys\n\n  # Add lineage storage to path to import our utility module\n\
    \  sys.path.append('storage/axiom')\n  from utils import sanitize_packing_comprehensive, get_objective_and_constraints\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Constructs a packing using multi-start with the 'trust-constr' optimizer\n      and saves the best result found to storage.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      \n      # Get objective and constraints from our utility module\n      objective, constraints = get_objective_and_constraints(n=n)\n      \n      bounds = [(0, 1)] * (2 * n) + [(0, 0.5)] * n\n      \n      best_score = -1\n      best_packing = (None, None)\n      num_trials = 40 # 'trust-constr' can be slower, so reduce trials slightly\n      start_time = time.time()\n      \n      for trial in range(num_trials):\n          if time.time() - start_time > 1000: break\n\n          np.random.seed(trial)\n          initial_guess = np.concatenate([np.random.rand(n, 2).flatten(), np.full(n, 0.05)])\n\n          # Using the 'trust-constr' method this time\n  \
    \        result = minimize(objective, initial_guess, method='trust-constr', \n                            bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints,\n                            options={'maxiter': 150, 'gtol': 1e-7, 'verbose': 0})\n          \n          if result.success and -result.fun > best_score:\n              best_score = -result.fun\n              current_centers = result.x[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n              current_radii = result.x[2*n:]\n              best_packing = (current_centers, current_radii)\n              \n              # Save the new SOTA result to lineage storage\n              np.savez_compressed('storage/axiom/sota_packing.npz', \n                                  centers=current_centers, \n                                  radii=current_radii)\n\n      if best_packing[0] is None:\n          # Fallback to a simple grid if no solution is found\n          grid_centers = np.array([[0.1 + (i%5)*0.2, 0.1 + (i//5)*0.2] for i in range(n)])\n          grid_radii = np.full(n,\
    \ 0.05)\n          return grid_centers, grid_radii\n\n      final_centers, final_radii = best_packing\n      \n      sanitized_centers, sanitized_radii = sanitize_packing_comprehensive(final_centers, final_radii)\n      \n      return sanitized_centers, np.maximum(sanitized_radii, 1e-9)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing, Planning, and Optimizing**\n\nOkay, so I just hit a new SOTA with my exhaustive multi-start SLSQP search – 2.6219759! That validates my hypothesis, and I'm really pleased with the modularization I achieved in the last turn. My immediate goal now is to keep improving the score and also document my findings, which is crucial research practice.\n\nRight now, I'm thinking I should:\n\n1.  **Prioritize Securing the SOTA:** Since I can't directly retrieve the optimal packing from the previous submission, I need to focus on *saving* it in the next run.  That means the next experiment is critical. I'll launch it now.\n2.  **Next Experiment:** The plan is a new submission. It will be almost identical to the last successful one, but with a few key changes:\n    *   Change `scipy.optimize.minimize` to `'trust-constr'` for the optimization method. This is a different algorithm and might get past local optima.\n    *   Add `np.savez` to save the `centers` and `radii` from\
  \ the best packing to `storage/axiom/sota_packing.npz`. This way, I'll have a perfect fallback.\n    *   Utilize my newly modularized `utils.py` for cleaner code and token efficiency.\n3.  **Refine Turn Strategy:**  I was originally going to document the results this turn.  However, system tips suggest I can go to private memory and update my log *while* the next submission is running. So, I will do that in the next turn.\n\nSo, for *this* turn, it's all about launching the `trust-constr` experiment with the save-on-success logic. Then, I'll update my research log and document the new SOTA.  I've checked the code, and everything looks good to go. This experiment seems well-rounded; a new optimizer, documenting the results, and the ability to save the data. Let's see what happens!\n"
