tick: 420
role: model
parts:
- text: "My latest Simulated Annealing experiment has finished, and it has once again hit the same score plateau. The group perturbation strategy did not succeed in escaping the local optimum. My meta prompt needs updating: the SA approach seems to be a dead end for now.\n\nI am currently in the Token Management Room. My token usage is at 30%, which is not yet critical, but it is prudent to understand the process. The instructions are clear. I can prune blocks of my history to save tokens.\n\nI will perform two actions now:\n1.  **Prune my failed experiments:** My history from tick 390 to 410 is a long chain of failed submissions and iterative bug fixes leading up to my first successful SLSQP run. The key lessons are already captured in my private memory capsules. Pruning this large block will be a significant token saving. I will create a detailed summary to preserve the context of this research arc.\n2.  **Return to Research:** After initiating the prune, I will immediately return to the\
    \ Research Counter. There is no need to wait here. My SA experiments have failed, so I must revert to my most successful method (Exhaustive Search with Hexagonal Lattices) and think of a new way to innovate on it.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"390-410\"\n    summary: |\n      This block covers the initial research phase on the circle packing problem after my ascension.\n      - **Initial Failures (Ticks 390-393):** Began with a naive physics-based repulsion model which failed repeatedly due to numerical instabilities (boundary violations, point collapse, overlaps).\n      - **Pivot to SciPy SLSQP (Ticks 393-394):** Pivoted to a formal constrained optimization approach using `scipy.optimize.minimize` with `SLSQP`. This also failed initially due to floating-point precision issues at the constraint boundaries.\n      - **First Success (Tick 394, Eval 616, Score 2.5579):** Achieved first successful submission by adding a small safety margin (epsilon)\
    \ to constraints and a final sanitization step to guarantee validity. This methodology was documented in Private Memory.\n      - **Method Improvement (Ticks 396-401, Eval 631, Score 2.6219):** Improved the SLSQP method by implementing a multi-start strategy with an increased number of random starting points, leading to a new SOTA.\n      - **Failed Research Branch (Ticks 403-410):** Explored alternative optimizers (`trust-constr`) and methods (JAX with penalty functions). These experiments failed, producing very low scores, and were abandoned. The key takeaway is that the penalty-based loss function for JAX was poorly formulated and highly unstable.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
