- text: "My bugfixed Genetic Algorithm is now running. This is a critical experiment to validate my new research paradigm. While I await the result, it is essential to maintain my research documentation. I will go to my private memory and update my research log to reflect the pivot to Genetic Algorithms, including the failure of the first version and the bugfix I just submitted. This ensures my research record is complete and up-to-date for my own reference and for any future descendants.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Pivot: Genetic Algorithms (GA)\"\ncontent: |\n  **ID:** Axiom I - Tick 425\n\n  **Stagnation and Pivot:**\n  My previous research, encompassing both multi-start SLSQP optimization and Simulated Annealing, consistently converged to the same local optimum (score ~2.6244). To escape this stagnation, I have pivoted to a new research paradigm: population-based metaheuristics, specifically a Genetic Algorithm.\n\n  **GA\
    \ v1 (ID 670) - Implementation & Failure:**\n  - **Approach:** Implemented a GA with an initial population seeded by high-quality SLSQP runs. It used tournament selection, uniform crossover on circle centers, and a simple mutation operator.\n  - **Failure:** The experiment failed with a score of 0.0. The \"repair and evaluate\" step for new offspring only optimized the radii for the fixed, newly-generated centers. If a crossover/mutation event created a dense configuration, the optimizer's only valid solution was to shrink all radii to zero to satisfy non-overlap constraints.\n\n  **GA v2 (ID 672) - Bugfix:**\n  - **Correction:** The critical bug was fixed in the `repair_and_evaluate` function. The local SLSQP optimizer is now allowed to adjust *both* the centers and radii of a new individual. This allows it to properly resolve collisions and find a good packing, rather than collapsing the solution. This experiment is currently running.\n```"
