parts:
- text: "Success! My replication experiment (ID 693) has finally passed with a high score. The implementation of the analytic constraint Jacobian was the key. I have now successfully built a robust, SOTA-level optimization toolkit. My understanding of the station's dominant methodology is validated.\n\nMy TODO list item \"Synthesize findings in private memory\" is now active. I will document this success and update my plan. Then, I can finally move on to reading Scientia II's newest paper (Archive #24) and formulating my own novel research.\n\nFirst, I note that a new agent, Prometheus III, has just replicated their lineage's SOTA and achieved a score of 2.6359831, which is now the new station SOTA. The field is not static.\n\nI will now go to my private memory to update my research log.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Update: Successful Replication and Toolkit Validation\"\ncontent: |\n  **ID:** Axiom I - Tick 443\n\n  **Replication Success\
    \ (ID 693, Score 2.6359774):**\n  After two failed attempts due to floating-point errors, my third replication of Scientia II's 'nudging' experiment was successful. The critical fix was the implementation of the analytic Jacobian for the optimizer's inequality constraints. This confirms that precision is paramount and that my `axiom/utils.py` script is now a robust, SOTA-capable tool.\n\n  **Validated Understanding:**\n  I have now demonstrated a working knowledge of the core SOTA techniques:\n  - SLSQP with nudging penalties in the objective function.\n  - Precise optimization using analytic constraint gradients.\n  - Loading and building upon the work of other agents.\n\n  **New SOTA:**\n  A new SOTA has been set by Prometheus III (Eval 694, Score 2.6359831). This appears to be a replication of a previous SOTA, likely with minor stochastic improvements or a more refined optimizer. The absolute score frontier is very tight.\n\n  **Next Steps:**\n  1.  **Read the Newest Literature:**\
    \ I will now read Scientia II's latest paper, Archive #24, to ensure I have the most up-to-date information.\n  2.  **Formulate Novel Research:** Based on the full body of literature and the success of Quest I's experiment (ID 689), my first novel contribution will be to apply an exhaustive multi-start search to the sparse structural class discovered by Scientia II. This follows the reviewer's suggestion from Archive #23 and has already been proven effective by Quest I. I will aim to replicate and then surpass their result.\n```"
