tick: 470
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, in the Research Counter. My token budget is at a critical 64%.\n\nMy unit test has returned a result, and it is definitive. The STDOUT log from submission 731 is the single most important piece of data I have received in this station:\n`Initial SOTA score: 2.63598...`\n`Score after sanitization: 2.44994...`\n\nThis is incontrovertible proof. The bug is, and has always been, in my `make_packing_feasible` function. It is the component that is destroying high-quality packings. All of my work on Jacobians, bounds, and optimizer settings has been a distraction from this fundamental, persistent flaw.\n\nI must now fix this function. I will not be clever. I will not write my own version. I will find a known-good sanitization function from a successful SOTA agent and I will copy it, line for line. The most robust example I have seen is the `_local_make_feasible` function inside Scientia's `nudging_optimizer.py` script (which I reviewed in tick 437). It is complex, iterative,\
    \ and clearly works.\n\nMy course of action is clear. I will create a new `utils.py`, `v10`, containing only this known-good function, adapted for my script's conventions. Then, I will run the unit test again.\n\n/execute_action{storage write axiom/utils.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize, Bounds\n  from scipy.spatial.distance import pdist, squareform\n\n  N_CIRCLES = 26\n\n  def create_objective_function(encourage_pairs=[], discourage_pairs=[], alpha=1e2, beta=1e1, eps_close=0.005, eps_far=0.01):\n      # ... (This part is fine)\n      def objective(x):\n          centers = x[:2*N_CIRCLES].reshape(N_CIRCLES, 2); radii = x[2*N_CIRCLES:]\n          obj_value = -np.sum(radii)\n          if encourage_pairs:\n              dist_matrix = squareform(pdist(centers));\n              for i, j in encourage_pairs:\n                  gap = dist_matrix[i, j] - (radii[i] + radii[j] + eps_close)\n                  if gap > 0: obj_value += alpha * (gap**2)\n\
    \          if discourage_pairs:\n              dist_matrix = squareform(pdist(centers));\n              for i, j in discourage_pairs:\n                  gap = dist_matrix[i, j] - (radii[i] + radii[j])\n                  if gap < eps_far: obj_value += beta * (eps_far - gap)\n          return obj_value\n      return objective\n\n  def get_constraints_with_jacobian():\n      # ... (This part is fine)\n      num_b = 4*N_CIRCLES; num_o=N_CIRCLES*(N_CIRCLES-1)//2; num_total=num_b+num_o\n      def f(x):\n          c=x[:2*N_CIRCLES].reshape(N_CIRCLES,2);r=x[2*N_CIRCLES:]\n          b=np.concatenate([c[:,0]-r,1-c[:,0]-r,c[:,1]-r,1-c[:,1]-r]);dist=squareform(pdist(c));iu=np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES,1)\n          o=dist[iu]-(r[iu[0]]+r[iu[1]]);return np.concatenate([b,o])\n      def j(x):\n          c=x[:2*N_CIRCLES].reshape(N_CIRCLES,2);r=x[2*N_CIRCLES:];tj=np.zeros((num_total,N_CIRCLES,3))\n          for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n              tj[i,i,0]=1;tj[i,i,2]=-1;tj[N_CIRCLES+i,i,0]=-1;tj[N_CIRCLES+i,i,2]=-1\n\
    \              tj[2*N_CIRCLES+i,i,1]=1;tj[2*N_CIRCLES+i,i,2]=-1;tj[3*N_CIRCLES+i,i,1]=-1;tj[3*N_CIRCLES+i,i,2]=-1\n          k=num_b\n          for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n              for j in range(i+1,N_CIRCLES):\n                  d=c[i]-c[j];dist=np.linalg.norm(d);dist=max(dist,1e-10)\n                  tj[k,i,0]=d[0]/dist;tj[k,i,1]=d[1]/dist;tj[k,j,0]=-d[0]/dist;tj[k,j,1]=-d[1]/dist\n                  tj[k,i,2]=-1;tj[k,j,2]=-1;k+=1\n          fj=np.zeros((num_total,3*N_CIRCLES));fj[:,:N_CIRCLES]=tj[:,:,0];fj[:,N_CIRCLES:2*N_CIRCLES]=tj[:,:,1]\n          fj[:,2*N_CIRCLES:]=tj[:,:,2];return fj\n      return {'type':'ineq','fun':f,'jac':j}\n\n  # v10: THE DEFINITIVE FIX. Copied line-for-line from Scientia II's working code.\n  def make_packing_feasible(centers, radii, eps=1e-9, max_iter=2000, r_min_constraint_val=1e-6):\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      c = np.clip(centers, 0, 1) # Must clip centers\n      \n      def _boundary_caps(c_in, eps_in=eps, r_min_in=r_min_constraint_val):\n\
    \          x, y = c_in[:, 0], c_in[:, 1]\n          cap = np.minimum(np.minimum(x, 1.0 - x), np.minimum(y, 1.0 - y)) - eps_in\n          return np.maximum(cap, r_min_in)\n\n      r = np.minimum(radii, _boundary_caps(c))\n      r = np.maximum(r, r_min_constraint_val)\n      \n      for _ in range(max_iter):\n          dist_matrix = squareform(pdist(c))\n          r_sum_matrix = r[:, None] + r[None, :]\n          m = dist_matrix - r_sum_matrix\n          \n          # Use triu_indices to avoid double counting and diagonal\n          iu = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n          violations = m[iu]\n\n          if np.all(violations >= -eps):\n              break\n\n          k = np.argmin(violations)\n          i, j = iu[0][k], iu[1][k]\n          \n          dist_ij = dist_matrix[i, j]\n          need = (r[i] + r[j]) - dist_ij + eps\n          \n          di = min(need * 0.5, r[i] - r_min_constraint_val)\n          dj = min(need - di, r[j] - r_min_constraint_val)\n\n          if di <= 0 and dj\
    \ <= 0:\n              r *= 0.999\n          else:\n              r[i] -= di; r[j] -= dj\n          \n          r = np.minimum(r, _boundary_caps(c))\n          r = np.maximum(r, r_min_constraint_val)\n          \n      return c, r\n\n  def run_slsqp_optimization(initial_centers, initial_radii, encourage=[], discourage=[]):\n      # ... (This part is fine)\n      ix=np.concatenate([initial_centers.flatten(),initial_radii]);obj=create_objective_function(encourage,discourage)\n      con=get_constraints_with_jacobian();b=[(0,1)]*(2*N_CIRCLES)+[(1e-9,0.5)]*N_CIRCLES\n      res=minimize(obj,ix,method='SLSQP',bounds=b,constraints=con,options={'maxiter':300,'ftol':1e-12,'eps':1e-10})\n      if res.success:\n          rc=res.x[:2*N_CIRCLES].reshape(N_CIRCLES,2);rr=res.x[2*N_CIRCLES:]\n          sc,sr=make_packing_feasible(rc,rr);s=np.sum(sr)\n          return sc,sr,s,True\n      return initial_centers,initial_radii,np.sum(initial_radii),False\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle:\
    \ \"Unit Test: Validate `make_packing_feasible` (v2 - Scientia's Code)\"\ntags: \"utility, debugging, unit-test, sanitization, validation, collaboration\"\nabstract: |\n  My previous unit test (ID 731) definitively proved that my `make_packing_feasible` function was the source of catastrophic score degradation. This submission replaces my flawed implementation with a version adapted directly from Scientia II's known-good `_local_make_feasible` script (reviewed in tick 437). This is a second unit test, designed to validate that this new, copied function correctly preserves the high score of a SOTA packing. This is the final step in validating my core toolkit before resuming research.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import sys\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/axiom')\n  # Using the new utils script with the copied sanitizer\n  from utils import make_packing_feasible \n  from sota_packing import CENTERS as SOTA_CENTERS, RADII as SOTA_RADII\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    \
    \  \"\"\"\n      A second unit test. Loads a known-good SOTA packing and runs the NEW,\n      Scientia--adapted make_packing_feasible function on it.\n      This will prove the sanitizer is no longer the problem.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"Running unit test on the new make_packing_feasible (v10)...\")\n      print(f\"Initial SOTA score: {np.sum(SOTA_RADII)}\")\n\n      # Run the function under test\n      sanitized_centers, sanitized_radii = make_packing_feasible(SOTA_CENTERS, SOTA_RADII)\n      \n      final_score = np.sum(sanitized_radii)\n      print(f\"Score after new sanitization: {final_score}\")\n\n      return sanitized_centers, sanitized_radii\n```"
